WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.257
Hi, I'm Axolotl!

00:00:02.257 --> 00:00:06.772
Husky claims up to four and a half times Apple's MLX on a Mac.

00:00:06.772 --> 00:00:08.707
Here's the mechanism behind that number.

00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:13.817
At batch one, every token streams all two point four gigabytes of

00:00:13.817 --> 00:00:15.435
weights through the memory bus.

00:00:15.435 --> 00:00:16.730
No engine reads faster.

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.643
So Husky gets more tokens out of each read.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.291
First, the ceiling for any engine that writes one token per read.

00:00:24.711 --> 00:00:28.341
MLX's median is six point two nine milliseconds a token.

00:00:28.761 --> 00:00:31.593
Husky puts the floor for a plain read at four point five.

00:00:32.197 --> 00:00:35.213
So kernels can win at most one point four times.

00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:39.435
Husky without a draft sits under that line, at one point one two five.

00:00:40.883 --> 00:00:42.181
Guess one: prompt lookup.

00:00:42.181 --> 00:00:45.750
If the last few tokens also appear in the prompt, propose

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.048
the seven that followed.

00:00:47.048 --> 00:00:48.346
No model, no training.

00:00:48.766 --> 00:00:53.505
Guess two: the Flash draft, a single small layer reading Woof's own hidden states.

00:00:53.505 --> 00:00:54.858
It proposes seven too.

00:00:55.278 --> 00:00:58.345
Woof checks every guess in one eight-row step, at about one and

00:00:58.345 --> 00:01:00.645
a half times the cost of a single row.

00:01:01.065 --> 00:01:04.947
It keeps the tokens it agrees with, so the output never changes.

00:01:04.947 --> 00:01:07.211
On edits, typically five or six survive.

00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:12.284
Across sixteen tasks: one point one two five without a draft, one

00:01:12.284 --> 00:01:14.234
point seven six with Flash on.

00:01:14.234 --> 00:01:17.485
The four point four eight headline is one edit row.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.594
At batch one, kernels can't outrun the bus.

00:01:21.594 --> 00:01:25.710
Speed comes from tokens per read, so compare speculation with speculation.

00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:30.712
To recap: the bus caps every read, and speculation fills each

00:01:30.712 --> 00:01:32.068
read with more tokens.

00:01:32.488 --> 00:01:34.720
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:35.358
I'm Axolotl.

00:01:35.358 --> 00:01:35.677
Bye!
